Per-layer analysis reveals encoder-specific gradient alignment patterns, consistent across Full OCC and Random OCC (Figure~\ref{fig:conflict_rate}). DINOv2 conflict rates hover near 50\% across all layers---statistically independent gradients consistent with direction-independent anti-freeze regularization. SigLIP early and middle layers similarly show $\sim$50\% conflict, but late layers (blocks 21--24) drop to 19--25\%, indicating active gradient cooperation in semantically rich layers. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, confirming they reflect architectural structure rather than label content. Auxiliary gradient flow thus operates through two complementary mechanisms: anti-freeze regularization in DINOv2, and semantic cooperation in SigLIP's late layers (Appendix~\ref{app:extended}).

